        "thinking": "I'm sizing the drape sampling step relative to the model's bounding box, roughly a centimeter for limb-scale geometry. I'm also reconsidering how crack paths are generated so they stay anchored to the surface rather than drifting off it—better to define cracks directly in surface coordinates using angular sweeps from a center point rather than relying on nearest-point projection.\n\nI'm working out a cylindrical helper that places crack points around a limb using an axial parameter and an angular offset, pushing them slightly outward so the nearest-surface drape logic snaps them correctly, and planning an analogous approach for the torso using the spine axis and ellipse radii with outward-offset sample points.\n\nFor the seam ribbon, I'm figuring out the sweep profile shape—small radius, few sides for a flat diamond-like cross-section—while worrying about frame twist along the draped path.\n\nLet me count triangles: with sides=4 and rings every centimeter along a 20cm crack, that's around 320 triangles per crack, times 40 cracks gives roughly 13k triangles, which seems acceptable for performance. I'll make the glow effect by layering a wider orange sweep plus a thinner yellow core, varying color along the sweep function.\n\nI'm settling on about 28 cracks total with both orange and yellow layers for richness, spacing rings no closer than the tube's edge length (~1.3cm given the radius). The Voronoi paint seams add extra surface detail without extra geometry cost.\n\nNow I need to write the plate placement function across limbs, then start assembling the full file with imports for the builder, IK, math, path utilities, and procedural texture helpers.\n\nWriting imports and type declarations...\n\nDefining the toon-shaded color palette for crust, obsidian, gold, and bone materials...\n\nWriting the lavaCrust paint function, which uses Voronoi cell distances to carve glowing seam lines (hot, mid, deep colors) between randomly varied-width crust plates, picking between three crust shades based on cell id otherwise.\n\nDefining plate outline shape...\n\nDefining plate function signature...\n\nI need to handle the edge case where the normal is vertical, since the fallback up vector could end up parallel to it.\n\nI'm also confirming that parts get auto-named, and figuring out how bone references should flow through for joint types. Now I'm sketching out the ball joint helper, using an icosahedron geometry with a glow effect anchored to the bone position.\n\nWriting collar function...\n\nSetting the collar's inner radius to clear the hex limb vertices, then moving on to designing a rune texture SVG with a ring, star, and glyph tick marks.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the tassel SVG orientation — since the card hangs upside-down when viewed from downstream, I need to draw the attachment end at the bottom of the drawing so the card reads correctly once hung with the root edge on top.\n\nWriting tassel SVG...\n\nNow I'm moving to the flame SVG with normal tip-up orientation and three layers, then the filigree decal and eye pieces. For the pauldron shoulder armor I'm working out the lathe geometry, defining tiered discs with tilted axes and centers offset outward from the shoulder joint.\n\nStill writing shoulder armor geometry...\n\nI'm also planning a glowing gem set into the pauldron's front-lower surface, plus a tassel hanging from the lowest tier's outer rim for extra detail.\n\nChecking the pauldron's placement against the head and neck, it seems the inner edge stays clear enough without overlapping, and the arm's path from shoulder to elbow passes naturally under the pauldron. Now I'm turning to the torso's front-facing details visible from camera angles -- paired chest plates, a central gem cage, ribbed ab plates, belt, loincloth, and hanging tassels -- working out how the front surface lofts at the chest height.\n\nWriting hit detection and plate placement logic...\n\nLeaving a thin molten center strip between plates, then adding lat plates on torso sides, big scapula plates and a central glowing rune disk on the back, plus flame plume and spine spikes. For legs, I'm placing thigh plates front-facing and outer-side plates at angled offsets, moving down toward calves next.\n\nShin guards get an obsidian hex dome kneecap, gold ring, calf rear plates, and a gold anklet collar, with magma balls peeking from the knee sides. Feet and fingers are handled, loincloth tabard sketched in, and I'm considering scattering small spike details on plates for wear, though that may wait for a later pass—also planning floating octahedra shards orbiting near the shoulders as elemental accents.\n\nI'm defining a hand construction function that builds the wrist and palm using frustum box geometry, setting width and height extents to shape the palm thickness and taper toward the fingers.\n\nFor the fingers, I'm calculating z-offsets to evenly space four fingers across the palm width, then shaping each finger segment with a combination of downward direction and slight forward curve to give a natural resting pose.\n\nWriting finger chains and sweeps...\n\nNow I'm positioning the thumb, offset from the palm edge and angled forward-down with two segments tapering from .055 to .04 radius.\n\nFor the fingers I'm adding glowing orange tips with a different crack-seeding pattern, then placing a knuckle plate on the back of the hand positioned near the wrist offset. For the arms I'm planning to reuse armor plates and compute body collision hits by sweeping along the arm joint chain from shoulder to wrist in one pass.\n\nFor the feet, I'm defining frustum boxes for the heel and front sections with tapering heights, aligning the foot center under the ankle joint, and adding three toe segments offset along the foot's width.\n\nStill writing toe geometry...\n\nNow I'm thinking about the pelvis root structure and adding decorative filigree decals to the largest plates like the chest and kneecaps.\n\nI'm working out a helper function to apply decals to plate parts, positioning them along the part's facing axis near the surface, confirming Part objects work directly as decal targets.\n\nFor the actual creature, I'm planning to use a single shared lava-crust paint across torso and limbs so the cell pattern reads as continuous, plus sketching out a pointed tabard outline and a belt loop sweep at the pelvis.\n\nNow I'm working out the buckle geometry and finding where the torso surface actually sits at a given height by casting rays forward and backward to locate the front and back faces precisely.\n\nI should check whether the torso loft section is rectangular or rounded before committing further details, so I'll render a quick preview first. Then I'm planning decal placements: a rune on the chest back with a gold ring around it, plus a neck collar with a pendant gem and eye decals on the head.\n\nNow I need to check how `glow.ts` handles arrays before wrapping the card frame meshes with it.\n\n",
